    "alert_id": "EX2_001",
    "title": "TDF and acute kidney injury: temporary hold, not permanent stop",
    "message": "Guidelines list acute kidney injury as a TDF toxicity and recommend substituting the responsible ARV for severe reactions. The patient has AKI (creatinine 2.4, was 0.9) on TDF. However, strong evidence favours a non-drug cause: 5 days of severe gastroenteritis with dehydration, two household contacts with the same illness, hypotension, tachycardia, and a urea-to-creatinine ratio of 24:1 (prerenal). Urinalysis shows no protein or glucose, which argues against TDF-induced tubular injury (Fanconi syndrome). TDF can worsen AKI and should be held temporarily, but permanent discontinuation may be premature if renal function recovers after rehydration.",
    "issue_type": "toxicity",
    "severity": "high",
    "recommended_action": "Hold TDF temporarily during acute illness and rehydration. Reassess renal function after recovery before deciding on permanent discontinuation or resumption.",
    "citations": [
      {"page_number": 270, "chunk_id": "hiv_guidelines_art.extraction__hiv_guidelines_art.extraction__p37_c1"},
      {"page_number": 267, "chunk_id": "hiv_guidelines_art.extraction__hiv_guidelines_art.extraction__p34_c2"}
    ]
  },
  {
    "alert_id": "EX2_002",
    "title": "DTG and TDF both require renal monitoring; no baseline creatinine documented",
    "message": "Guidelines require serum creatinine monitoring for patients on TDF, including at baseline and periodically. The only prior creatinine on record is from 2025-02-14 (375 days ago), and no creatinine was documented at the 2025-08-20 visit despite the note stating 'creatinine normal.' This leaves a 12-month monitoring gap. The current AKI (creatinine 2.4) underscores the importance of regular renal monitoring on TDF. The clinician's plan does not include scheduled renal reassessment after the acute episode resolves.",
    "issue_type": "monitoring_gap",
    "severity": "moderate",
    "recommended_action": "After acute management, establish a renal monitoring schedule with serum creatinine at recovery, then at 3-month intervals while on TDF.",
    "citations": [
      {"page_number": 260, "chunk_id": "hiv_guidelines_art.extraction__hiv_guidelines_art.extraction__p27_c2"}
    ]
  },
  {
    "alert_id": "EX2_003",
    "title": "Plan lacks ART continuity strategy during TDF hold",
    "message": "The clinician plans IV fluids and empiric antibiotics but does not specify how ART will be managed during the acute illness. If TDF is held, 3TC and DTG alone constitute an incomplete regimen risking virological failure. Guidelines state that for severe toxicities, the responsible medicine should be substituted following specific substitution guidance rather than simply removed. The patient is virally suppressed (<50 copies/mL, 2025-08-20), so preserving suppression is critical. The plan should address either temporary full ART interruption or an appropriate substitution.",
    "issue_type": "plan_review",
    "severity": "high",
    "recommended_action": "Specify ART management: either substitute TDF with an alternative agent (e.g., TAF or ABC) or provide clear guidance on temporary interruption duration and reinitiation.",
